result.reasoning:
Same mechanism: both freeze the backbone and train a lightweight scorer only by KL divergence to an aggregate of the layer's own attention (head-averaged or head-summed mass). Both use exact top-k of that target as the reference and swap the learned scorer in at inference. B's differences are details within that family. Its 4-token blocks with one pooled key per block, few query projections and gated sum of rectified dot products are an indexer-style scorer. Its block-max target is another way to aggregate attention. Its only model is a hybrid with softmax attention at every fourth layer, which A's scope already covers. Neither design changes what is being learned or how.

Why B is stronger: A is a useful but conventional measurement. Splitting the loss into exact top-k versus dense, learned versus exact top-k, and block-shared versus per-query selection is how sparse-attention indexers are routinely evaluated. A states no sharp hypothesis, and any result is just a set of numbers. Its strengths are breadth (4K to 128K, three budgets, hybrid coverage) and engineering value, not identification. B isolates a specific and plausible failure mode: a distilled selector may learn surface or script-bound matching that its target does not need, so it fails to transfer to writing systems it never saw in training. Its design controls confounds well:
- a difference-in-differences against the selector's own target, which removes budget effects;
- recall counted only on passage blocks sharing no content token with either question, and only for questions without named-entity or number anchors;
- a purely literal selector to check the lexical control in both directions;
- writing systems seen versus unseen in training, with transliteration and same-script translation as comparators;
- pre-registered headroom and grounding gates before any difference is interpreted;
- explicit conditions for calling a result negative.
Either outcome is informative. B's weaknesses are a narrow scope (one context length, one budget, one model type, evidence recall rather than end-task quality) and the real chance of a null result. Even so, it is more novel and better identified, while A largely repeats evaluations that already exist for learned sparse-attention selectors.

